{:edb/db-before-t 1193 :edb/db-after-t 1194 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534506 50 #inst "2026-10-12T01:06:41.957Z" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186194249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194251 13194139534506 17592186045417 false] [17592186194249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194253 13194139534506 17592186045417 false] [17592186194249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194254 13194139534506 17592186045417 false] [17592186194249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423368 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186194249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423372 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186194249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423418 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186194249 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423368 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186194399 1078 #edb/ref 17592186194401 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423443 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423445 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423448 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423450 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423447 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423449 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423451 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423452 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423442 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423444 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423446 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423453 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198493 1050 1020.0 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198603 1071 "![](images/e6/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x, y, z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$." 13194139534506 17592186045417 false] [17592186198603 1071 "![](images/e6/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198603 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198628 1071 "![](images/6b/6b0c9a89f2768561274d66416b1f5c30fd3fad808ea8d311773fad9272fa1692.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x + 6yz^{2}, 0, 0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$." 13194139534506 17592186045417 false] [17592186198628 1071 "![](images/6b/6b0c9a89f2768561274d66416b1f5c30fd3fad808ea8d311773fad9272fa1692.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186198628 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨x+6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/a9/a900f65b194e7992d3de6abfb078ff0c7cd1e2d163861d9ade810409d632693d.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=2$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨x+6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2+6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2+6y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2+6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2+6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2y+3{y}^{2}{z}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2+3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[2z+{z}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨x+6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-6y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{z}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3+(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186199469 1064 "![](images/d2/d2a5641e502b11cca4f469ec743adc62d614f669d61c1b2d0b0c7549fb60bf6b.png)\n\n \n\nGiven a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin, an outward-pointing unit normal vector to the sphere at the point $P(x,y,z)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can sometimes use this to calculate the flux of a vector field through a sphere. Let's see an example." 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186199469 1064 "![](images/d2/d2a5641e502b11cca4f469ec743adc62d614f669d61c1b2d0b0c7549fb60bf6b.png)\n\nGiven a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin, an outward-pointing unit normal vector to the sphere at the point $P(x,y,z)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can sometimes use this to calculate the flux of a vector field through a sphere. Let's see an example." 13194139534506 17592186045417 false] [17592186199479 1064 "Similar to the case of *closed* curves, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" a closed *surface*. \n\nFor closed surfaces, the convention is the positive orientation is always given by the *outward-pointing* unit normal vector.\n\n![](images/d2/d2bb07e4684cfc3a8c5641ecc6930dbc1c76027a735c7efe0c1c60cd17a91060.png)\n\n \n\nWhen calculating the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a closed surface, we always measure the flux relative to the surface's positive orientation.  \n\nLet's see an example." 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186199479 1064 "Similar to the case of *closed* curves, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" a closed *surface*. \n\nFor closed surfaces, the convention is the positive orientation is always given by the *outward-pointing* unit normal vector.\n\n![](images/d2/d2bb07e4684cfc3a8c5641ecc6930dbc1c76027a735c7efe0c1c60cd17a91060.png)\n\nWhen calculating the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a closed surface, we always measure the flux relative to the surface's positive orientation.  \n\nLet's see an example." 13194139534506 17592186045417 false] [17592186423367 1080 "8" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423367 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423367 1083 #uuid "022cc252-1b2d-53c8-b8f4-7e0fa0c560d5" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423368 1080 "4\\pi " 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423368 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423368 1083 #uuid "04de0393-ca1b-597d-bacc-969f4123ee27" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423369 1080 "-9" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423369 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423369 1083 #uuid "06134337-3698-5e99-b304-3c524f8d58ba" 13194139534506 1759218