 1069 "q-161216" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423442 1071 "![](images/d3/d333ab14f8a42bb1d40894421afc76ff60acbb6c4cb858ddee5f6e8a39be2d15.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $1$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423442 1072 #uuid "0aa0eff9-8245-5a13-9648-df63821931fe" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423442 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/51/5193451b39b03b11cf9780fd7039360ba8fb5de5876a3593f7a5dadde2e7be5b.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-1}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-0}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+(-8\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423438 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423443 1069 "q-161218" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423443 1071 "![](images/07/0785d19e62e5b317bbaee924fa8b6b303c2bbd10ab338357e6a88846b1d5b1d1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423443 1072 #uuid "348cfda2-2f1c-590f-874e-27ed4639164d" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423443 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/fd/fdaad0039f4b7fea5c6e301f4cc3b89687f5f4081e3bd87f8ce31daacda2744b.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{2}-2y\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}-2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-2xy]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}9-6y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[9y-3{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}9\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-9[y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6+(-18) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423433 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423444 1069 "q-162541" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423444 1071 "![](images/58/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨(3-x){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423444 1072 #uuid "541f1701-1958-5d60-973d-d311e60b511f" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423444 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨(3-x){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/00/0072a53c42916c095c625cebd0a0564b92dcac5469f9d7452e9c75ef7e7d7158.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=3$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(0,y,z)=⟨(3-3){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨(3-x){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3-0){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-3{e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-3{e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(y,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =-3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1) \\\\  & =3\\pi (1-{e}^{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+3\\pi (1-{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =3\\pi (1-{e}^{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423440 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423445 1069 "q-161217" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423445 1071 "![](images/83/838fe13d4ad778c391ff628b508d17ed450b56e800180c946038be1ccfc5655e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423445 1072 #uuid "5dff93a1-4655-5852-a4ef-0e50149062ad" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423445 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/d4/d47102fe8d01acf93fe0e6ef9017e06412e843ea64584b9f4e7151214aeb9197.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+6⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x-y+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x-y+6\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-y+6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-y+6\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy+6x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{13}{2}-y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{13}{2}y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}y-x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y-x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =11+1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423441 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423446 1069 "q-162542" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 1319