.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,x-2+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =x+2{z}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(2{z}^{2}-2)x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{z}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}z]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,x+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-(x+2{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(x+2{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2x{z}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{2}z+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{3}{2}+18) \\\\  & =-\\frac{39}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}+(-\\frac{39}{2}) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423439 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423449 1069 "q-161231" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423449 1071 "![](images/e7/e73d17583a6aa15601abd957f25cfb11ded37159649a67c5b237915d03ed98f7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-z,\\,y,\\,z+x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $2$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423449 1072 #uuid "9c176acf-06da-52d5-8022-73333152fefe" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423449 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-z,\\,y,\\,z+x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨x-z,\\,y,\\,z+x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x(x-z)+{y}^{2}+z(z+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-xz+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423432 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423450 1069 "q-161220" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423450 1071 "![](images/e8/e8708a16a0f6db347acf2c1a82a602d2fa8ce60d4bb8369f1414eca3465e4e71.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423450 1072 #uuid "b265113a-f521-5f72-933b-1132d7a6f831" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423450 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/8e/8ed6ffdeab54bc6888d245ef4f1c050c31e010c67dfa93aea365fc6cb523e382.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy-4x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y-3\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-3y]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}2x+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+(-\\frac{15}{2}) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423430 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423451 1069 "q-161239" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423451 1071 "![](images/e7/e73d17583a6aa15601abd957f25cfb11ded37159649a67c5b237915d03ed98f7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $2$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423451 1072 #uuid "c6c92f89-f1d7-5cd5-9f3c-fab3133854ae" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423451 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x(x-3z)+{y}^{2}+z(z+3z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-3xz+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423437 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423452 1069 "q-161240" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423452 1071 "![](images/e6/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3x+4y,\\,3y-4x,\\,3z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423452 1072 #uuid "cea56a0f-1b32-5284-84a0-25dd8320e070" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423452 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x+4y,\\,3y-4x,\\,3z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =x(3x+4y)+y(3y-4x)+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+4xy+3{y}^{2}-4xy+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =12\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423434 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423453 1069 "q-162539" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423453 1071 "![](images/14/14b1144f52dcacc094901a159bf54c04f00daccc85df7b117b2163ed327dcd4f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423453 1072 #uuid "ec4a413a-1560-589f-a588-2fce65f680ae" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true] [17592186423453 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/61/614037b52ca1f60f03420019891af1004227e2e0f3b4a89d71adbdc68dadf1dd.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=2$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(y,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(0,y,z)=⟨0\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534506 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-022cc252-1b2d-53c8-b8f4-7e0fa0c560d5" 17592186423367 "answer-04de0393-ca1b-597d-bacc-969f4123ee27" 17592186423368 "answer-06134337-3698-5e99-b304-3c524f8d58ba" 17592186423369 "answer-08051b4d-e523-5380-b21a-12e9c0665d2c" 17592186423370 "answer-0a4abdcc-7659-5905-b6ff-d499f42fc74f" 17592186423371 "answer-0ba5ee31-bb54-534a-8802-752dbe4f750e" 17592186423372 "answer-0ce314fb-b190-50fc-b3d0-c8253bbb95a3" 17592186423373 "answer-184fb30f-a682-5501-8386-eb41dfbe9220" 17592186423374 "answer-19ccd5aa-3240-5023-a265-5b3b645f65d1" 17592186423375 "answer-1a03aa61-a6a4-59bd-9047-ec009e7bb552" 17592186423376 "answer-1a061d5d-bd6b-5f69-86e4-81f1ce29315f" 17592186423377 "answer-1c85b770-7d4d-5c9c-ba3a-e0c18db05700" 17592186423378 "answer-24e66adf-5d3d-50f8-bd05-476213ea7079" 17592186423379 "answer-253e9383-49a8-53b0-8d24-6a8dbb0d3f09" 17592186423380 "answer-2b26cc63-426c-571e-9b86-42c72dd116be" 17592186423381 "answer-454b87df-9358-5e83-88c0-07680f41c488" 17592186423382 "answer-4a77d700-dee1-58de-add2-b15b7a553b7b" 17592186423383 "answer-4b538de2-0bf9-52bf-9303-9311b62ebd0c" 17592186423384 "answer-4f178a77-2414-5